      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm trying to set up some reorder logic for my shop. we sell small electronics components and i'm worried about running out of lithium battery cells during our peak season. can you give me a basic formula or some rules to help me figure out how much safety stock i should keep on hand to avoid stockouts?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A common approach for safety stock calculation is the \"Economic Order Quantity\" (EOQ) method. This formula takes into account the desired service level, lead time, maintenance costs, and the size of your inventory.\n\nThe basic formula is:\nSafety Stock = (Desired Service Level / (1 - Desired Service Level)) * Lead Time * Standard Annual Demand\n\nYou can also consider the \"Successful Order Frequency\" method:\nE = Total Demand / Average Number of Items on Hand\n\nFor advanced calculations, you can use the \"Hillier & Coffing\" formula, which takes into account lead time, demand variability, and commit limits.\n\nConsider using historical data to determine your actual lead times, demand variability, and service levels for better accuracy.",
